The sequence $x_n$ converges to $c$ in $\ell_1$, meaning $\|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$. For any $p > 1$, the $\ell_p$ norm of $x_n - c$ satisfies $\|x_n - c\|_p \leq \|x_n - c\|_1$ because each term $|x_i - c_i|^p \leq |x_i - c_i|$ when $|x_i - c_i| \leq 1$, and only finitely many terms exceed 1 (since the $\ell_1$ norm is finite). Thus, $\|x_n - c\|_p \to 0$, implying convergence in $\ell_p$.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{Yes}

